Both loops below are O(n). One runs multiple times faster on real hardware purely due to memory layout — no algorithmic difference at all:

```c
// Cache-friendly: contiguous array, sequential access
struct Point { float x, y, z; float color; };
Point points[100000];

float sum_x_aos(Point* pts, int n) {
    float total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += pts[i].x;  // pulls a full 16-byte struct per x needed
    return total;
}

// SoA layout: only the field you need is contiguous
float xs[100000], ys[100000], zs[100000], colors[100000];

float sum_x_soa(float* xs, int n) {
    float total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += xs[i];      // every fetched byte is used
    return total;
}
```

`sum_x_soa` typically runs several times faster than `sum_x_aos` for large n, because it wastes none of each fetched cache line on unused fields, and the sequential access pattern lets the CPU prefetch aggressively and often auto-vectorize the loop. Both are Θ(n) — this is a constant-factor win, invisible to complexity analysis, decided entirely by memory layout.
